WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.690
The best actors often infuse their characters
with facets of their own personalities.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:09.349
Sure, that can lead to better performances
overall - but it can also lead to emotionally

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:15.259
draining scenes and, in some cases, identity
crises for actors who dove a little too deep

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:16.309
into their characters.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:17.670
"Jake, I don't know who I am anymore!"

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:18.720
Justice Smith in The Get Down

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.250
The Get Down might have fallen victim to its
own massive budget and co-creator Baz Luhrmann's

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.380
busy schedule, but the show's portrayal of
the birth of hip-hop - and its depiction of

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.199
a mythical New York City - was a delight while
it lasted.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:36.110
The show's intense commitment to world-building
was also reflected in the actors, who practiced

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.629
choreography on set in a reconstructed Queens
neighborhood, and even involved some of the

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:42.600
actors wearing period-appropriate underwear.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.600
"Yeah.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.600
You can go too, if you need to, man."

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.600
"No.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.600
No.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.600
If I'm gonna rhyme, I need a beat.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.890
One that don't retire just 'cause Ma's making
Rice-a-Roni with the real cheese and the chili

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:51.890
ground meat."

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.320
As series lead Ezekiel Brown, actor Justice
Smith committed to the role with gusto for

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.899
the long hours of filming.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:02.789
Unfortunately, that dedication to craft ended
up taking an emotional toll on the young actor

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:06.970
when his method acting techniques started
to erase the distinction between himself and

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:08.700
the character he was playing.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.910
Smith told Vulture:

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:13.580
"The lines get blurred and you're like, 'What
is me and what is the character?

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.300
Am I really like this or is that just him?'

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.420
My biggest fear was, 'What if I don't come
back?

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.450
What if I don't return to who I am?'"

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.450
Dean Norris in Breaking Bad

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.360
In season 3 of AMC's dark drama Breaking Bad,
Walter White's DEA brother-in-law Hank is

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.450
nearly shot to death, and he winds up crippled
for part of the next season.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.390
The character's former upbeat attitude and
macho dialogue is replaced with sullen glares,

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:38.510
moody silences, and a frankly stank outlook.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:39.510
"Hank."

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:46.610
"I leave this hospital when I walk outta here,
you understand?

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.990
And not before."

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.460
This continues episode after episode.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.630
Oh - and that mineral collection…

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.980
GOD that mineral collection…

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.310
Actor Dean Norris found himself increasingly
enveloped in the newly grim outlook of his

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.450
character, which necessitated weeks of filming
for the arc.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.510
According to Entertainment Weekly, Norris
admitted:

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:10.649
"You are constantly dark for 15‑hour days,
being just depressed as a character and being

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:12.530
mean to my [onscreen] wife.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.879
I was just constantly in this kind of pissed-off
mood.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:17.519
[The] whole Season 4 for me was not pleasant."

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:21.599
Still, Norris' dedication to his craft paid
off in the long run.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:25.879
Afterall, if Hank never took time off to recover
from his injuries, we wouldn't have comedic

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:26.879
gems like this.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:28.500
"Did you check them for damage?"

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.500
"They're rocks, Hank."

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.500
"No, they're minerals.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.500
Jesus, Marie.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.719
I got some geodes coming that are very delicate,
alright?"

00:02:34.719 --> 00:02:38.019
Laverne Cox in Orange Is the New Black

00:02:38.019 --> 00:02:40.840
While Orange Is the New Black might have its
comedic moments, it's also never shied away

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.349
from the deeply unpleasant realities of imprisonment
and sexism.

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.319
Following a group of women imprisoned in a
women's correctional facility, the show features

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:53.890
the various intersections between race, class,
gender, and sexuality.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:59.780
In season 3, a feud finds Sophia, a trans
woman, sent to solitary confinement ostensibly

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.370
for her own protection after a violent attack.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.370
"Jesus, Burset.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.139
Why do you have to make everything so hard?"

00:03:07.139 --> 00:03:11.129
"No offense, but f--- you.

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:12.129
Sir."

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:16.859
Actress Laverne Cox, herself transgender,
admitted that although the storyline was important

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:20.050
to showcase, it was deeply upsetting to film.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.050
She told Vulture:

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.670
"When I read this episode, I was just in tears.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.840
I bawled reading it because it was deeply
triggering for me […] As trans people of

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.500
color, there's so many different layers of
that.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.389
So the trauma of that was just very real and
raw for me."

00:03:33.389 --> 00:03:34.389
Jack Gleeson in Game of Thrones

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:38.260
There are plenty of loathsome villains in
the history of television, but very few have

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:43.239
been quite as young and quite as twisted as
King Joffrey on Game of Thrones.

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:47.330
Even among a cast full of violent monsters,
the young king stood out as a truly repugnant

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:48.849
and sadistic character.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:51.999
"I won't hurt him.

00:03:51.999 --> 00:03:54.219
Much."

00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:58.629
Actor Jack Gleeson seems to be as kind-hearted
as his character is not, since he's gone on

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:02.930
record as feeling very conflicted over the
violent acts committed in the show.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.379
As Gleeson said in an interview with Rolling
Stone:

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:09.381
"You tend to abstract yourself from the creepiness
of it when you're playing it, but when you

00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:13.950
see it on television, it sends shivers down
my spine […] I would like to try and defend

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.060
him, but I would have a pretty hard job doing
it."

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.510
Gleeson even retired from acting after his
time on the show ended - and while we're sad

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.790
to see the young and promising actor fade
from the field, we never get tired of watching

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:26.920
his character's "retirement."

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.390
Krysten Ritter in Jessica Jones

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:32.100
While Krysten Ritter's got a bit of a knack
for playing self-destructive characters like

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.411
Chloe on Don't Don't Trust the B-- in Apartment
23 or Jane in Breaking Bad, her performance

00:04:37.411 --> 00:04:41.350
in Marvel's Jessica Jones is arguably one
of her best.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:45.690
The show follows the titular superpowered
private detective as she struggles with trauma

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.600
in a world of unbreakable men and manipulative
monsters.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.850
Playing a character that deals with such dark
experiences required Ritter to really invest

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:59.090
herself in Jessica Jones' loneliness, which
involved a difficult transition.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.840
Ritter told the Los Angeles Times:

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.231
"It definitely takes a toll because I'm more
bubbly and alive and zestful and I like to

00:05:05.231 --> 00:05:06.500
have a good time.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.500
So after a while, you're like, 'Oh my God,
I'm getting really depressed.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:13.200
And I just had to move across the country,
so I'm in an apartment that doesn't have my

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.200
furniture.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.380
I'm not around my friends.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.540
I spent a lot of time walking around doing
the scenes by myself in my apartment."

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:24.390
Yeah, that sounds like a real bummer - but,
hey, if Ritter's self-imposed isolation led

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:29.080
to her performance as the ever-badass Jessica
Jones, then it may have all been worth it

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.830
in the end.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.590
"Smile."

